  through the characters and the plot you  know what's the take home message when  we're all done here in the case of boku  no hero after over 300 chapters now it's  quite clear that the central moral  question that horikoshi is exploring is  both surprisingly basic and complex at  the same time  what does it mean to be a great hero  but this question implies a lot more  than you might think because the story  explores this problem by contrasting two  polar viewpoints individualism and  collectivism in other words what does it  mean to be a great hero on the personal  level and what does it mean to be a  great hero from the viewpoint of society  right from the start we're told that  midoriya wants to become the greatest  hero that saves everybody you know a  statement so vague and so shonen that  i'm 95 sure that he recall she chose it  on purpose so he could put his own twist  on it and so at the same time we also  get introduced to the society that  similarly seems to be as straightforward  and simple as it gets you know the  peaceful citizens are protected by the  good heroes from the bad villains  painting this quirk society as a  peaceful utopia only that everyone has  superpowers  [Music]  and despite everyone having a unique  work here this society is normal when  everyone's special  no one's special and when you take the  moment to really think about it for a  second the wording that hirokoshi uses  here is also very clever the story about  heroes and villains sets our  expectations for these groups as starkly  as humanly possible at the beginning  especially as in the first couple of  arcs both groups fulfill these  expectations pretty much perfectly  villains do bad things and the heroes  stop them to save and protect everyone  you will never find  a more wretched hive of scum and villain  and yet it doesn't take all too long  until you realize that this initial  black and white view of the boku no hero  world is not reality but we are  literally sharing midoriya's faulty  world view for a large part of the story  midoriya thinks that being a great hero  means saving people being assaulted by  bad villains that cause all the pain and  suffering in this otherwise perfect  society  [Music]  and so his major flaw as a character is  his fanatic idealization of this hero  world that is projected on the number  one hero almight that midoriya almost  worships like a god  so now you might ask why exactly does  deku think that way well in general i  think that this society in itself seems  to propagate this simplified image of  good and evil as well the deeper answer  can be found with the deuterogenist of  the story  deku has endured verbal and physical  abuse from bakugou for over 10 years  he's 15 now so that means for over  two-thirds of his life in this society  that is shaped and defined by quirks and  that lives under the guard of the hero  profession  deku is one of the rare cases where  quirk has not manifested in other words  he's just a normal human being like you  and me which makes him relatable only  that in this society of superpowers this  makes him the true outcast  now being a very kind-hearted and  altruistic person by nature that wants  to help people  having no quirk put so much distance  between him and his dream of becoming a  hero that deku had no other choice but  to either completely idolize or detest  them the fact that bakugou kept treating  him like a piece of trash for not owning  a quirk manifested such a strong sense  of low self-worth and insecurity within  him that when he finally got the chance  to actually become a hero against all  odds he embraced this one thing he  always wanted as all good and  all-powerful  completely incapable of seeing its flaws  i mean you know even after bakugou kind  of stopped constantly picking on deiku  you still see him twitching and  stuttering whenever he's around him  again and again taking away from the  confidence that midoriya slowly builds  over time by mastering one for all and  fighting for and with his friends i  honestly believe that this is quite  often overlooked but as he says himself  in this panel here midoriya truly hates  bakugou for this he admires him ever  since they were young yes but he hates  him for the way he treated him and it is  the low sense of self-worth that bakugou  beat into him over and over and over and  over that makes deku blind to the  reality around him idolizing the hero  profession so much that he kept putting  his health and his life on the line to  such an absolutely ridiculous and  unhealthy extent that it quickly became  worrying to everyone around him  and so while the impulsive and  egocentric bakugou has to learn to be  more considerate of others something  that i will discuss in detail in his  analysis the neurotic and altruistic  midoriya has to learn to respect himself  more and make harder decisions in order  to actually be able to save others i  think the perfect example of this  insight beginning to dawn on him is when  izuku can't save bakugou as he has  already sacrificed both his arms  [Music]  because for the longest time proving  himself to be worthy of inheriting one  for all and becoming a hero is way more  important to him than his own well-being  even if it also means being less  effective and useful to others not a  very smart move now while midoriya's  need for self-realization and maturity  is quite evident early on in the story  it takes quite a while to reveal the  other aspect of midoriya's flaw the  ambiguous and dark side of the hero  society  however more and more you learned that  this world where good heroes save the  innocent citizens from the evil villains  is a lot more morally gray and complex  than you originally thought and so as so  often is the case with apparent utopias  and stories it turns out that it's the  hero society itself that produces its  villains in the first place as big  surprise most people aren't actually  born evil but become who they are as the  result of their circumstances yes  there's also some explorations of the  concept nature versus nurture in boku no  hero but primarily hirokoshi focuses on  external circumstances  why does everything have to be  complicated exploring what makes people  into villains is a major aspect of the  story that is explored in a number of  ways going as far as making the villains  the main character of certain story arcs  and the central symbol for this is of  course none other than shigaraki tomura  the anti-deku whom we follow on his path  to becoming the strongest villain  firstly we understand that in this  society of quirks there are two  fundamental flaws both of them are  explored over and over again the first  of these is the notion that people with  destructive or dangerous quirks are  treated as outcasts and incompatible  with society now this is of course a  pretty straightforward metaphor for  general racism in the real world  discrimination based on physical  attributes one has absolutely no control  over  no matter if it's  twice at some point we get that these  people don't have terrible quirks  because they're villains no they are  villains because they have terrible  quirks an absolutely fantastic example  for this is arie who mirrors tomorrow's  story almost completely with the only  exception that the heroes actually end  up coming saving her as they failed to  do with tomura now the second flaw of  the hero society is the profession of  heroes itself that we already know is  far beyond all good and noble but  additionally has eradicated the common  sense of compassion and altruism in the  society at large creating a sort of  common bystander syndrome  this point is especially pronounced in  tomorrow's backstory instead of helping  this lost child people simply assume  that some hero will do the job not  bothering to do anything themselves and  so the message here is that ultimately  it's the society of heroes themselves  that creates most of these villains by  failing to do what midoriya idealizes so  much about them  saving everyone  now this is interesting because as you  know midoriya and tomorrow of course  mirror each other extremely closely with  deku himself being a victim of  discrimination and neglect only that he  gets taken in by the hero all might  while tomorrow gets taken in by one for  all don't forget that tomorrow also  wanted to be a hero and yet midoriya is  only starting to recognize very very  slowly this truth about the world that  the society of heroes that he has  thought to be so perfect is not so  perfect after all and that brings me to  daegu's character arc and what actually  makes him a true hero even if it takes  quite a while in the story to get there  the payoff however is just the better  feels good  feels good  really good real real real good as a  result of these two character flaws no  self-worth and blinding idealization of  the hero worlds what emerges only very  slowly but very clearly throughout the  story is that midoriya's arc is about  understanding the complexity and faults  of society while at the same time  growing past his personal flaws to truly  become a hero that can save everyone  even no especially someone as apparently  unredeemable as tomura who by mere  chance was forced to take the path  opposite to izuku and this arc is in  full progress now especially in his more  recent encounters with tomorrow midoriya  is starting to see the world for what it  is yes heroes help and are very  important to keeping the balance in a  world full of dangerous superpowers but  the system is neither perfect nor  unfailable  [Music]  similarly he's starting to grow as well  as a person as with the mastering of his  abilities and all the setbacks he had  from going overboard with his  self-sacrificial attitude he has started  to mature fight smarter think more about  himself and overall starts to form his  own opinions and beliefs about what it  means to be a great hero and i think  this slow emancipation from all might  and the other heroes he always admired  is really important because imitation is  a strong theme within the story over and  over we've shown that trying to be  exactly like someone always leads to  tragedy and failure  [Music]  instead horikoshi promotes the idea that  taking inspiration from those you admire  is great as long as you walk your own  path and find your own way of doing  things we see this with ida who tries to  be like his brother with todoroki and  his mother twice and all of his clones  and of course deku and bakugo who both  try to imitate their respective ideal of  all might no matter which of these  countless examples you take they always  end in failure imitating someone else  brings suffering not self-realization  [Music]  and so ironically enough it is deku's  incredibly pure and straightforward  character that changes the minds of many  of these people ida todoroki etc and  finally deku also starts to realize this  more and more himself almight wasn't  perfect as he also couldn't save  everyone instead deku starts going his  own path unlocking new powers of one for  all while realizing that saving everyone  also includes those that the current  society has abandoned i think the  kickoff to this development is this  moment right here where midoriya  realizes that instead of punching like  all might it might probably suit him way  better to actually kick  genuine goodness and desire to help  others his immeasurable courage and  ability to change the hearts of those  around him that make him a true hero and  while it understandably might be  frustrating at first to follow him as a  protagonist who starts from a very  very negative point on his character arc  the more the story and deku progress the  bigger the payoff for actually sticking  with him become

